{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791542275,"nonce":"A2g4v2yUlzqCqwrWWGWtxPGVXgw=","message":"Committed d516e99: Bound AIR caches and share interpreter bodies.\n\nThe measured retention was redundant ownership, not GC finalizers:\n- The SSA walker cloned both Optimized.ir and Optimized.ser, cloned the serialization's pc maps again, copied inline sites, eagerly analyzed BTreeSet liveness for every function, and leaked all prepared bodies/modules.\n- OPTIMIZED pinned all configurations forever; AshModule kept every lowered callee indefinitely. Finalized IR and serialization retained pass-growth Vec capacity.\n\nFix:\n- SSA Prepared owns the canonical Arc\u003cOptimized\u003e. A compact u32 instruction-index list skips Pos markers without cloning/mutating the IR or dispatching markers. Source positions and original serialized pc indices are preserved.\n- Rc ownership keeps active calls valid across cache growth, forget and hot reload, then releases retired bodies. The SSA module is owned instead of leaked. Construct the type shim without cloning/discarding raw opcodes and object metadata.\n- OSR liveness and usize register types use OnceLock and are built only for an actual transfer.\n- Finished IR, nested operands and serialization shrink their spare capacity before caching.\n- Strong optimized-body reuse is bounded by an 8 MiB conservative owned-storage budget; lowered callees by 2 MiB per module, shared across views. Weak entries recover a live canonical body after eviction. Oversized entries are not strongly retained. Cache keys also include bytecode identity. Racing inserts adopt the winner.\n- The serialized walker pins its canonical owner to preserve positional identity when the global cache evicts it.\n- ASH_AIR_CACHE_STATS reports live canonical storage, strong residency, hits/misses/evictions and IR/serialized storage separately. These estimates are not RSS.\n\nSettled TreeGc allocation census, identical bytecode/HDLLs, Cranelift only, MallocStackLogging=lite, capture after 22s, both scripts completed and selected src:\n- baseline e3dd679: AIR-root malloc allocations 50.72 MiB; SSA-prepare root 18.20 MiB; other 23.91 MiB\n- d516e99 sources: AIR-root 25.86 MiB; SSA-prepare root 0.03 MiB; other 27.61 MiB\n- total live malloc allocations excluding the untouched GC side-table reservation: 117.79 -\u003e 78.32 MiB, a 39.47 MiB reduction\n- profiler physical footprint: 226.2 -\u003e 187.1 MiB\nRoot classification shifts with compiler inlining: the new 0.03 MiB SSA-root bucket does not mean the walker's owned type/debug/index data is zero; some appears under other. The net total is the comparison to use.\n\nCanonical cache census on this run: 867 live bodies, 16,783,225 estimated bytes (IR 10,911,969; serialization 5,871,256), strong residency 7,373,343 bytes \u003c 8,388,608 budget, 259 evictions. Live executable interpreter bodies are separately owned; they are not duplicate strong reuse entries.\n\nNormal TreeMotion application-preset runs, order before/after then after/before:\n- baseline physical footprint 175.66 and 202.33 MiB (mean 188.99)\n- after 171.99 and 170.06 MiB (mean 171.02), mean reduction 17.97 MiB\n- idle CPU \u003c0.024% in all four samples; frame-work p95 1.2-1.4ms\nPhysical footprint is noisier than logical allocation ownership and does not promise a 39 MiB RSS reduction on every run.\n\nValidation:\n- 203 AIR tests pass, including optimized O3 fixture round trips.\n- 3 cache eviction/racing-insert/oversize ownership tests pass.\n- SSA invalidation lifetime test passes and verifies OSR metadata is not eagerly built.\n- Ashui's existing pixel suite passes.\n- Exact/normalized results match the saved baseline for stdlib, exceptions, cross-tier throws, f32, closure-call and DeltaBlue fixtures in interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM modes (18 comparisons).\n- cast_in_try 3/3, f32 parity 1/1, retyped_reads 2/2, OSR hot-loop counter parity 1/1 pass.\n- Existing macOS 316cce8 forced-snapshot gate still has a routing/timing failure: enters LLVM OSR directly, so no Cranelift-to-LLVM taken marker. One saved-baseline rerun took that path; the new rerun did not, with identical correct answers. Raising only the diagnostic opt threshold to 1,000,000 exercised source=osr on the new build and preserved all 3M counters, pointer state, float sum and phis. The original gate is not reported as passing.\n\nArtifacts in ashui (ignored diagnostic output):\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/heap-112154/ (baseline)\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/heap-112239/ (after)\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/air-pair-112522/report.json\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/air-behavior-112304/report.json\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/retier-baseline-112852/\n- .ashui/memory-comparison/retier-controlled-113152/\n\nThe unbounded strong cache and duplicated SSA ownership are fixed. Further storage opportunities remain: prepared bodies kept for future interpreter fallback after promotion, and serialized opcodes retained alongside IR for flat consumers (the census's serialized storage includes both opcodes and OSR/source maps). This issue stays open to track that remaining retirement/representation work rather than calling all AIR retention eliminated.","files":null}]}